{"kind":"assert","text":"Addendum to stop 8656d9ee. Option (a) fails. A written-plan entry can reach 128 distinct foreign sequences.\n\nFrom source:\n- The written-plan shells (push, read, receipt and evidence-plan) are outcomes. An outcome copies its origin's uses (packages/derive/src/settle.ts:243).\n- The widest origin is the `reserve` delivery (packages/platform/src/destination.ts:372-387, v3 at :587-592). Its fields name these foreign facts:\n  - operation and manifest;\n  - up to 64 review-verdict facts;\n  - up to 64 request-check facts and 64 decidedBy facts;\n  - up to 32 report facts from issue lanes;\n  - in v3, up to 64 propose-file facts.\n  The scope caps them at usesPerEntry (delivery.ts:257).\n- Only OWNER (destination.ts:186) ties a fact to a sequence, and it ties only `operation` to the sender lane. A verdict, job, source or manifest fact from another change scope is not refused. The lane rule only marks it not sound (destination.ts:1551).\n- So 128 later uses from about 128 sequences can occur under destination@17.\n\nThe choice is wider than (b) or (c), because births are real bytes at runtime. Today a worst-case reserve outcome fits in 256,474 bytes. With births it needs 304,730 bytes, and the runtime entryBytes cap refuses it. Option (b) keeps v17 admitted, but its forecast then promises less than the runtime enforces. This is the mismatch that point 4 of 28295c20 forbids for the use count.\n\nOptions now:\n(b) Keep the v17 arithmetic. Accept that a reserve of more than about 128 - 2 x (48,256 / 362) foreign facts is refused at runtime by entryBytes and not at admission. State the real threshold in the note.\n(c) Charge births in full. destination@17 is not admitted until a replacement exists.\n(d) A new destination version, in a later head and not in the cutover: reserve refuses change facts from scopes other than the sender lane. That bounds the sequences to about 35 (1 change lane, 32 issue lanes, the claim and the directory), so births fit. The cutover then takes (b) or (c) until (d) lands.\n\nI recommend (b) for the cutover head, with the exact runtime threshold measured and written in the note and a failing-at-admission test deferred to (d), and I recommend (d) filed as its own request. With (c), the destination's evidence path is off from the cutover deploy until (d), and the demo uses that path. Your ruling decides. The lane does not change the forecast until then."}